1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:06,384
A backup copy of the heeyID database leaves the bank, on a disk that nobody should have taken.

2
00:00:06,434 --> 00:00:12,384
On it are the customers of the bank: names, passport data, addresses and phone numbers.

3
00:00:12,434 --> 00:00:14,256
Aziz is one of them.

4
00:00:14,306 --> 00:00:18,128
What can the person with the disk read?

5
00:00:18,178 --> 00:00:24,080
In Lesson 7, each adapter held a secret, and the platform kept passports encrypted.

6
00:00:24,130 --> 00:00:29,336
This lesson shows where every key lives, and why the disk is worth so little.

7
00:00:29,386 --> 00:00:33,800
All the keys of heeyID live in Vault, and the Vault belongs to the bank.

8
00:00:33,850 --> 00:00:39,272
Each zone has its own new Vault, and the security team of the bank keeps the keys that unseal it.

9
00:00:39,322 --> 00:00:41,048
The vendor never sees one of them.

10
00:00:41,098 --> 00:00:46,040
I think this is the line in the design that matters most: the bank keeps the keys.

11
00:00:46,090 --> 00:00:47,912
Six kinds of keys sit there.

12
00:00:47,962 --> 00:00:50,864
Walk across the disk, and meet them one by one.

13
00:00:50,914 --> 00:00:57,104
First, the verified identity of Aziz: his names, his document data and his addresses.

14
00:00:57,154 --> 00:01:01,808
His record has its own data key, and that data key encrypts the record.

15
00:01:01,858 --> 00:01:08,288
The data key is stored next to the record, but wrapped: the installation key-encryption key encrypts it.

16
00:01:08,338 --> 00:01:11,240
The key-encryption key never leaves Vault.

17
00:01:11,290 --> 00:01:19,232
To read the record, the gateway sends the wrapped data key to Vault, gets it back unwrapped for this one request, and then forgets it.

18
00:01:19,282 --> 00:01:21,152
This is envelope encryption.

19
00:01:21,202 --> 00:01:26,456
On the disk, the thief has records and wrapped data keys, and no way to open them.

20
00:01:26,506 --> 00:01:29,720
And Vault records each time a key is unwrapped.

21
00:01:29,770 --> 00:01:31,352
Second, search.

22
00:01:31,402 --> 00:01:37,160
An operator must find Aziz by his exact personal identification number, among all the rows.

23
00:01:37,210 --> 00:01:38,720
But the number is encrypted.

24
00:01:38,770 --> 00:01:45,320
So the platform keeps a blind index: a keyed hash of the number, made with the installation index key.

25
00:01:45,370 --> 00:01:49,808
The same number always gives the same index, so an exact search works.

26
00:01:49,858 --> 00:01:53,960
Without the index key, the thief cannot even test a guess.

27
00:01:54,010 --> 00:01:58,352
The same key makes the digest that binds a session to its device.

28
00:01:58,402 --> 00:02:00,272
Third, the one-time codes.

29
00:02:00,322 --> 00:02:06,680
heeyID keeps a code only as a digest, and the digest mixes in a secret pepper from Vault.

30
00:02:06,730 --> 00:02:12,464
A short code is easy to guess, so a plain digest of it is easy to reverse.

31
00:02:12,514 --> 00:02:16,208
With the pepper in Vault, the thief cannot even check a guess.

32
00:02:16,258 --> 00:02:17,768
Fourth, the audit trail.

33
00:02:17,818 --> 00:02:22,976
Each audit event is chained to the event before it with an HMAC, under the audit key.

34
00:02:23,026 --> 00:02:25,448
Change one event, and the chain breaks.

35
00:02:25,498 --> 00:02:30,128
A daily verifier checks the chain, and raises an alert at each break.

36
00:02:30,178 --> 00:02:32,480
Fifth, the token signatures.

37
00:02:32,530 --> 00:02:35,960
Keycloak signs each token with the realm keys.

38
00:02:36,010 --> 00:02:40,280
They sit in the Keycloak database, encrypted with a secret from Vault.

39
00:02:40,330 --> 00:02:50,408
Sixth, the credentials of the outside services: the MyID credential, the TAF client secret, the client certificates of the adapters, and the push credential.

40
00:02:50,458 --> 00:02:53,888
Each credential goes only to the adapter that uses it.

41
00:02:53,938 --> 00:02:59,696
The push credential, for example, arrives as a file for the push adapter, and for nothing else.

42
00:02:59,746 --> 00:03:03,560
None of them is ever in the code, on a phone, or in a log.

43
00:03:03,610 --> 00:03:05,432
And the passwords?

44
00:03:05,482 --> 00:03:08,192
They are not encrypted at all, on purpose.

45
00:03:08,242 --> 00:03:14,120
heeyID keeps only a slow, salted password hash, and a hash cannot be reversed.

46
00:03:14,170 --> 00:03:17,960
There is no key to steal, because there is nothing to decrypt.

47
00:03:18,010 --> 00:03:19,832
Keys also age.

48
00:03:19,882 --> 00:03:23,072
Each key has a rotation period, and the bank sets it.

49
00:03:23,122 --> 00:03:31,136
When the key-encryption key rotates, its old versions stay for unwrapping, and the platform wraps the data keys again in the background.

50
00:03:31,186 --> 00:03:34,952
Old audit keys stay too, so old chains still verify.

51
00:03:35,002 --> 00:03:40,928
Each key has an owner who approves its change: the security team of the bank, or its platform team.

52
00:03:40,978 --> 00:03:42,800
Back to the disk.

53
00:03:42,850 --> 00:03:51,152
The thief has encrypted records, wrapped data keys, blind indexes, code digests, password hashes and a signed audit chain.

54
00:03:51,202 --> 00:03:54,656
Every key that could open them stays in the Vault of the bank.

55
00:03:54,706 --> 00:03:58,496
The disk is a loss of hardware, not a loss of identities.

56
00:03:58,546 --> 00:04:02,024
To recap: one Vault for each zone, in the bank.

57
00:04:02,074 --> 00:04:11,840
Six kinds of keys: the key-encryption key, the index key, the code pepper, the audit key, the realm keys, and the credentials of the outside services.

58
00:04:11,890 --> 00:04:18,296
Envelope encryption for identities, a blind index for search, and digests for codes and passwords.

59
00:04:18,346 --> 00:04:21,680
The keys never leave the bank, and they never reach a phone.

60
00:04:21,730 --> 00:04:25,928
But the apps of the bank still keep something on the phone, and still call heeyID.

61
00:04:25,978 --> 00:04:28,712
What may an app keep, and how does it talk to heeyID?

62
00:04:28,762 --> 00:04:31,232
Next: the API contract.
